meta {
  name: Upload with malformed digest header
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/upload
  body: multipartForm
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  X-Message-Digest: not-a-valid-hex-digest
  Content-Type: multipart/form-data
  Accept: application/json
  x-correlation-id: {{$guid}}
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ADMIN_ACCESS_TOKEN}}
}

body:multipart-form {
  file: @file(fixtures/statement1.pdf) @contentType(application/pdf)
  accountNumber: 123456789
  date: 2025-11-07
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

tests {
  test("rejects a digest header that isn't 64 hex characters", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(400);
    expect(res.getHeader('content-type')).to.include('application/problem+json');
    expect(res.getBody().errorCode).to.equal('INVALID_INPUT');
  });
}

docs {
  Verified against the running service: this returns errorCode INVALID_INPUT, not INVALID_MESSAGE_DIGEST.
  The generated AdminApi interface already carries @Pattern(regexp = "^[A-Fa-f0-9]{64}$") on the X-Message-Digest
  header parameter itself, so a malformed digest is rejected by jakarta bean validation (ConstraintViolationException
  -> GlobalExceptionHandler -> INVALID_INPUT) before the controller body - and therefore before
  ValidationUtil.validateMessageDigest - ever runs. The format-check branch that throws InvalidMessageDigestException
  (INVALID_MESSAGE_DIGEST) in ValidationUtil appears to be unreachable dead code via real HTTP requests; only the
  digest-mismatch branch (see the sibling "Upload with digest mismatch" request) is actually exercised.
}
